index ce4a998e..ba503fb6 100644
--- a/HUMANLIKE.md
+++ b/HUMANLIKE.md
@@ -87,6 +87,12 @@ interval rather than a fixed one.
 | A moving target does not take the walk apart | `process/FollowProgress.java`, `FollowProcess#walkOrTurnAround` | Following used to cancel the path every time the goal stepped clear of where it ended - a stop every second or two at the target's own walking pace. The walk under way now keeps going while its end is still a step toward the thing being followed (closer to it than the player is), and the plan-ahead chains the next stretch to wherever the goal has wandered by then. Only somebody who has actually gone somewhere else - doubled back, run off sideways - makes the bot stop, turn around, and path from where it stands. |
 | Somebody being followed is faced | `FollowProcess#regardWhileStanding` | While the feet have nowhere to go - close enough already, or waiting on a way - the head is turned to the nearest of whoever is being followed, as a standing target: the turn at the hand's own speed with its beat before it, the sway at half, the idle glance off it and home again, and a slow track as they move about. The regarded entity stays selected across scans until it leaves the valid follow set, so two nearby matches do not make the head flick between faces when their distances cross. A walking tick's movement sets its own target afterwards and wins. A person following somebody who stops turns to them; one who goes on facing the last way they walked is a dog on a lead. |
 
+## Working a job
+
+| what | where | the rule |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| The builder works outwards from where it stands | `process/PlaceSearch.java`, `BuilderProcess#searchForPlacables`, `BuilderProcess#toBreakNearPlayer` | Both halves of the builder's tick look for their block in the same order: the columns of an eleven-by-eleven box around the player's feet, nearest first, so the block picked is the one the person is standing next to and can see rather than the one at the far corner of what they can reach. It used to be walked `x` from the west edge inwards, then `y`, then `z`, which anchored the order to the world's axes instead of to where the person was standing - a wall came down from a corner and went back up from wherever the player happened to be, in the same tick, and the head swung the width of the box and back for every block. Placement was changed to work outwards in `32308640`; breaking was left on the old loops until now. The two ranges are the search's own and are not shared: the break box reaches one block below the feet (only with `breakFromAbove`) to five above, the placement box five below to one above, and the block the player is supported by is never mined. The order is a fixed list rather than anything measured from the player's exact position, because the builder walks towards the block it picked and a choice that shifts under it would be reversed half way there - columns equally far away keep the order the loops used to produce them in, which is what a stable sort leaves them in. Order only: the same blocks are considered, the same ones turn out to be reachable, and nothing about what gets built or what a path costs has moved. |
+
 ## Deliberate exceptions
 
 - **Elytra flight** sets the aim straight to its target. It is a control loop: the flight solver
